# Separation of duties, structurally (playbook Stage 5 control point).
#
# The identity that authored a change cannot approve it. Branch protection requires
# a code owner's approval; the agent's machine identity is in no team here, so it can
# open a pull request and can never approve one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ON ADOPTION, replace every owner below with your real teams. The commented lines
# show the intended shape — four distinct accountabilities, not one catch-all:
#
#   *                           @your-org/platform-team
#   /policy/                    @your-org/platform-team @your-org/second-line
#   /intent/                    @your-org/product-owners
#   /specs/                     @your-org/product-owners @your-org/architecture
#   /service/app/v1_legacy/     @your-org/architecture
#
# Second line on /policy/ is the one people skip. It is also the one that makes the
# control mapping in policy/controls.yaml something second line has agreed to rather
# than something engineering asserted.
#
# An owner that does not resolve is silently ignored by GitHub: the rule looks
# configured and requires nobody. scripts/check_codeowners.py fails the build on that,
# which is how this file came to be pointing at a real identity instead of four teams
# that do not exist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

